# Phase 7 — Automation and CI (Part 1: GitHub Actions)

## Goal
Set up a real Continuous Integration pipeline: automatically run the
full pytest suite on every push to `main`, closing the gap in the
project's delivery pipeline where broken code could otherwise reach the
Linux staging VM without ever being verified.

## What I did
- Learned YAML as a new configuration format, indentation-based
  structure, the same underlying idea as Python's own indentation
  rules, but used for configuration rather than code.
- Created `.github/workflows/ci.yaml`, the specific, required location
  GitHub Actions watches for workflow definitions.
- Built the workflow from scratch, piece by piece:
  - `on: push: branches: [main]`, the trigger.
  - `jobs: test: runs-on: ubuntu-latest`, a fresh, disposable machine
    GitHub provisions on demand for each run.
  - Four steps in sequence: check out the repository's code
    (`actions/checkout@v4`), install a specific Python version
    (`actions/setup-python@v5`), install dependencies
    (`pip install -r requirements.txt`), and run the test suite
    (`pytest -v`).
- Pushed the workflow, watched it run live in GitHub's Actions tab, hit
  a real failure, diagnosed it from the raw job logs, fixed it, pushed
  again, and watched a second run go fully green.

## Why
A CI pipeline is the automated gate this project didn't have before,
the point in the pipeline between "code written locally" and "code
pulled onto the Linux VM" where correctness actually gets verified,
without relying on a human remembering to run tests by hand every
time. It also produces a real, visible signal (a pass/fail badge on
every commit) that any outside reviewer can trust at a glance.

## What went wrong and what I learned

### Building the YAML (structural and conceptual mistakes)
- **Mixed up `apt` and `pip`.** Wrote `sudo apt pip install -r
  requirements.txt`, conflating the Linux system package manager with
  Python's own package manager, two completely separate layers.
  Corrected to the plain `pip install` command already used dozens of
  times on the Linux VM.
- **Tried to run the interactive CLI instead of the test suite.**
  Wrote `python3 main.py` as the final step, which is the exact script
  that crashed with `EOFError` back in Phase 5 when it had no
  interactive terminal to read from, a GitHub Actions runner has no
  terminal either, so this would have reproduced that same crash for
  an unrelated reason. More fundamentally, running the *application*
  was never the goal, CI exists to run the *tests* that verify the
  application, a different tool entirely (`pytest`).
- **Collapsed three steps into one by only using a single `-`.** Each
  item in a YAML list needs its own `-` marker; without it, everything
  underneath gets read as belonging to one step, producing invalid
  duplicate keys (`name:` and `uses:` each defined multiple times
  within what YAML saw as a single mapping).
- **Referenced the wrong thing with `uses:`.** Initially pointed it at
  my own GitHub username/repo, misunderstanding that `uses:` references
  a separate, reusable, *published* action (in this case, GitHub's own
  official `actions/checkout`), not the project's own repository.
- **Guessed an action version that doesn't exist** (`v7` for
  `setup-python`), and separately, conflated "the version of the action
  I'm using" with "the version of my own project," when it's actually
  the same concept as pinning `bcrypt==5.0.0` in `requirements.txt`,
  a version of a dependency, unrelated to my own code's history.

### The real CI failure, diagnosed from actual logs
- The first real run failed with `Process completed with exit code 2`,
  a generic summary that required digging into the actual job log to
  understand.
- Traced it to: `test_main.py` imports `fastapi.testclient.TestClient`,
  which now hard-requires a package called `httpx2` (the same thing
  flagged as a soft deprecation warning weeks earlier while testing on
  the Linux VM, apparently since become a strict requirement). This
  dependency had been dropped during an earlier `requirements.txt`
  cleanup, since it's only needed for *testing*, not for the app to
  run, so it wasn't obviously "missing" from a runtime perspective.
- Added `httpx2` back to `requirements.txt`, pushed, and the second run
  succeeded end to end in 28 seconds.

# Phase 7 — Automation and CI (Part 2: Bash Automation Scripts)

## Goal
Build two Bash scripts that automate real operational tasks for the
RBAC project: a backup script that protects the live `users.json`
credential store, and a health-check script that verifies the whole
service chain (nginx → uvicorn → FastAPI) is actually functioning, not
just running.

## What I did
- Wrote `scripts/backup_rbac_users.sh`:
  - Generates a sortable, filename-safe timestamp with
    `date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S`.
  - Copies `users.json` into a dedicated `/var/backups/rbac_users/`
    directory (kept separate from the project repo on purpose, backups
    of a credential store don't belong in version control) as
    `users_backup_<timestamp>.json`.
  - Cleans up backups older than 7 days with
    `find ... -mtime +7 -delete`.
  - Proved the retention logic actually works, not just assumed to
    work, by manually backdating a test file with `touch -d` and
    confirming the cleanup command deleted it while leaving recent
    backups untouched.
- Wrote `scripts/health_check_rbac.sh`:
  - Uses `curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}"` to hit the API and
    capture only the numeric HTTP status code.
  - Checks `http://localhost/docs` (FastAPI's built-in docs page)
    rather than `/`, since the app has no route defined at the root
    path, that's the first thing I checked before landing on this.
  - Reports "Healthy" on `200`, "Unhealthy" otherwise.
  - Proved it catches a real failure, not just the happy path, by
    stopping `python-rbac.service` with `systemctl stop`, rerunning the
    script to confirm it correctly reported "Unhealthy" (a `502`, not a
    `200`), then starting the service back up.
- Made both scripts executable (`chmod +x`) and symlinked them into
  `~/.local/bin`, a personal-scripts directory I added to `$PATH` via
  `~/.bashrc`, so either script runs as a bare command from anywhere on
  the VM instead of needing a full path or `bash` prefix.
- Committed and pushed both scripts to this repo directly from the
  Linux VM, which required configuring `git config --global user.email`
  and `user.name` for the first time on this machine.

## Why
`users.json` is untracked in git (correctly, since it holds bcrypt
password hashes and lockout state) and lives only on the VM's disk, so
without a backup it's a single point of failure. A health check exists
because "the process is running" and "the service actually works" are
not the same claim, a hung or misconfigured app can pass a
`systemctl status` check while still failing every real request.

## What went wrong and what I learned

### Backup script
- Forgot the `$` sigil in `echo "timestamp"`, printing the literal word
  instead of the variable's value, a direct callback to basic
  variable-expansion rules.
- Multiple wrong attempts at the `date` format string: missing the `+`
  prefix entirely, a stray space between `+` and the format, using the
  week-based year (`%G`) instead of the calendar year, and including a
  colon-producing field (`%R`), which would have broken the filename.
  Landed on `+%Y%m%d_%H%M%S`.
- Introduced a stray `)`, a missing `sudo`, and a wrong variable name
  (`$back_path` instead of the actually-defined `$backup_path`, which
  would have silently expanded to nothing) in the `cp` line, then
  repeated the same typo category (`$backp_path`) plus a missing
  `sudo` again in the `find` cleanup line.
- `ln -s` failed with "No such file or directory" because
  `~/.local/bin` didn't actually exist yet, an earlier `mkdir -p` step
  had been skipped. Also learned this VM doesn't auto-add
  `~/.local/bin` to `$PATH` the way some distros do, had to add it to
  `~/.bashrc` manually and `source` it.
- Got a `Permission denied` running the script by bare name, since it
  had only ever been run via `bash scriptname.sh` before, which
  doesn't require the execute bit. Fixed with `chmod +x` on the real
  file, the symlink's own permissions don't matter, only the target's
  do.
- First-ever `git commit` from this VM failed with "Author identity
  unknown", since git had never been configured with an identity on
  this machine before. Fixed with `git config --global user.email` and
  `user.name`.

### Health check script
- First version checked `http://localhost/` and got a `404`, which
  briefly looked like a failure but actually confirmed the opposite,
  nginx and uvicorn were both up and responding, the app just has no
  route defined at the root path. Switched to checking `/docs`, a route
  guaranteed to exist by default in FastAPI.
- On the first attempt to test the failure path, stopped and
  immediately restarted the service without running the health check
  in between, so nothing was actually verified. Had to redo it in the
  correct order (stop → check → start) to actually observe the script
  catch a real failure.
- Confirmed a `502` (nginx up, can't reach uvicorn) is a distinct,
  meaningful failure signature from a `404` (both up, wrong route) or a
  connection refused (nginx itself down), useful for knowing what
  "unhealthy" actually means in different failure modes.

## Evidence
$ bash backup_rbac_users.sh
20260913_171226
$ ls -l /var/backups/rbac_users/
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root ... users_backup_20260913_171226.json
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root ... users_backup_20260913_180501.json

$ bash health_check_rbac.sh
Healthy: http://localhost/docs returned 200

$ sudo systemctl stop python-rbac.service
$ bash health_check_rbac.sh
Unhealthy: http://localhost/docs returned 502
$ sudo systemctl start python-rbac.service

$ git push
d268db6..6f6ef6d main -> main
